---
title: "Baby's first language: lexer and parser"
date: 2026-09-14
---
First post in a series walking through how [invariants](https://github.com/ujaandas/invariants)' compiler actually works, phase by phase. If you haven't read [the overview post](/writing/invariants): it's a small DSL for describing structured data and the relationships between its fields, compiled ahead of time into a schedule that ends up driving an LLM's token generation. This series is about the compiler itself, not the LLM part.

If you want to actually build one of these yourself at some point, the resource I'd point you to first is [Crafting Interpreters](https://craftinginterpreters.com/) by Bob Nystrom - it's the book I actually learned this from, it's free online, and basically everything in this post is the same shape of idea, just applied to a much smaller, much weirder language than the one in the book.

Let's start at the very front: how does a string of source text turn into something a compiler can actually reason about?

```
spec CloudProvision {
    field base_ram: Integer {
        value >= 8;
    }
}
```

There are two jobs here, done by two completely separate pieces: the **lexer** turns that raw text into a flat list of tokens, and the **parser** turns that flat list into a tree. Let's do both, in order.

### Step 1: the lexer

The lexer's only job is to walk the source character by character and decide "what is this a piece of." It doesn't know anything about specs or fields or what's structurally valid - it just recognizes chunks.

Feed it `value >= 8;` and it should spit out four tokens: an identifier (`value`), an operator (`>=`), a number (`8`), and a semicolon. The way you actually do this is boring almost by design: you look at the current character, decide what kind of token it could be starting, and consume characters until that token is complete.

Keywords are the one part worth calling out, because the trick is not to special-case them in the scanner at all. You scan any run of letters as a plain identifier first, and only afterwards check whether that identifier happens to match something in a keyword table:

```cpp
inline static const std::unordered_map<std::string_view, TokenType> keywords{
    {"spec", TokenType::KW_SPEC},
    {"field", TokenType::KW_FIELD},
    {"invariant", TokenType::KW_INVARIANT},
    {"IN", TokenType::KW_IN},
    {"this", TokenType::KW_THIS},
    // ...
};
```

So `spec` and `CloudProvision` go through the exact same code path in the scanner - it's only after scanning that one of them turns out to be a keyword and the other stays a plain identifier. One code path instead of two means one less place to get out of sync.

By the end, `value >= 8;` becomes a flat list: `[IDENTIFIER(value), GREATER_EQUAL, NUMBER(8), SEMICOLON]`. No structure yet, no idea that `value >= 8` is a comparison feeding into a constraint - just "here are the pieces, in order."

### Step 2: the grammar

Before writing the parser, you need to know what you're actually trying to parse *into*. This is where a [formal grammar earns its keep](https://github.com/ujaandas/invariants/tree/main/docs/lang_grammar).

The statement grammar is the scaffolding - `spec`, `field`, `invariant`, and nothing else:

```
spec
    = "spec", IDENTIFIER, "{", { spec_member }, "}";

field
    = "field", IDENTIFIER, ":", type, "{", { constraint }, "}";

constraint
    = expression, ";";
```

The expression grammar is where all the actual computation lives - arithmetic, comparisons, boolean logic - and it's written as a chain, each rule built out of the one below it:

```
expression   = implication;
implication  = disjunction, [ "->", implication ];
disjunction  = conjunction, { "||", conjunction };
conjunction  = equality, { "&&", equality };
equality     = comparison, { ( "==" | "!=" ), comparison };
comparison   = membership, { ( ">" | ">=" | "<" | "<=" ), membership };
membership   = term, [ ( "in" | "not in" ), term ];
term         = factor, { ( "+" | "-" ), factor };
factor       = unary, { ( "*" | "/" | "%" ), unary };
unary        = ( "!" | "-" ), unary | postfix;
postfix      = primary, { postfix_op };
primary      = literal | IDENTIFIER | "this" | list | "(", expression, ")";
```

This chain *is* the operator precedence table. `implication` sits above `disjunction`, which sits above `conjunction`, all the way down to `primary` at the bottom, and the ordering isn't arbitrary - it's exactly "loosest-binding operator first, tightest-binding last." `->` has to be the loosest, because you want `a || b -> c || d` to group as `(a || b) -> (c || d)`, not something weirder. `*` has to bind tighter than `+`, so it sits below `term`. Read top to bottom, the grammar already tells you the precedence order - you don't need a separate table anywhere.

### Step 3: the parser

In recursive descent, every rule becomes a function, and every function calls the rule below it before it looks at its own operator:

```cpp
ExprPtr Parser::expression() { return implication(); }

ExprPtr Parser::implication() {
  ExprPtr left = disjunction();
  if (match(TT::ARROW)) {
    ExprPtr right = implication();
    return std::make_unique<Expr>(
        BinaryExpr{std::move(left), BinaryOp::Imply, std::move(right)});
  }
  return left;
}

ExprPtr Parser::disjunction() {
  ExprPtr left = conjunction();
  while (match(TT::LOGICAL_OR)) {
    ExprPtr right = conjunction();
    left = std::make_unique<Expr>(
        BinaryExpr{std::move(left), BinaryOp::Or, std::move(right)});
  }
  return left;
}
```

Notice `implication` doesn't know or care what a disjunction actually contains - it just asks `disjunction()` to go figure that out and hand back a fully-formed subtree, then checks if there's an `->` sitting after it. Every level in the chain does exactly this: delegate downward first, then handle your own operator if there is one.

Let's trace `value >= 8` through this by hand, since it's small enough to do fully:

1. `expression()` calls `implication()`.
2. `implication()` calls `disjunction()`, which calls `conjunction()`, which calls `equality()` - none of these see an operator they recognize yet, so each just delegates straight down.
3. `equality()` calls `comparison()`.
4. `comparison()` calls `membership()`, which calls `term()`, which calls `factor()`, which calls `unary()`, which calls `postfix()`, which calls `primary()`.
5. `primary()` is the first function that actually consumes something: it sees the identifier `value` and returns it as a leaf node. That bubbles back up through `postfix`, `unary`, `factor`, and `term` untouched, since none of them see an operator they own.
6. Back in `comparison()`, having gotten `value` back from `membership()`, it now checks the next token - `>=` - matches, so it consumes it, calls `membership()` again for the right-hand side (which bottoms out and returns `8`), and wraps both into a single `BinaryExpr{value, GreaterEqual, 8}`.
7. That comparison node bubbles all the way back up through `equality`, `conjunction`, `disjunction`, and `implication` untouched, since `>= ` was the only operator in the whole expression, and it becomes the final tree.

Thirteen functions deep, and only one of them - `comparison` - actually did anything, because it was the only precedence level with a matching operator in this particular expression. Everything else was pure pass-through. That's the entire trick: most of the chain doesn't cost you anything at runtime beyond a function call, and you get correct precedence for free just by getting the order of the chain right once, up front.